WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.560
These are the films that arrived in&nbsp;
theaters to the sound of a sad trombone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.040
stunk up the joint for a while, and then slunk&nbsp;
away in shame. The next time you're having an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.160
off day on the job, just be glad your job doesn't&nbsp;
get released to thousands of screens worldwide.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.680
The Valiant Comics property Bloodshot, about an&nbsp;
ex-Marine who is raised from the dead to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.280
a nanotech-infused super soldier, seemed like it&nbsp;
had a chance to be a pretty good Vin Diesel movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.760
Unfortunately, two things went wrong for&nbsp;
Bloodshot on the way to box office glory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.640
For one thing, it was released on March 13th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.280
right as theaters everywhere began to&nbsp;
close down. Secondly, it wasn't very good.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.960
Observers also bemoaned the film's reliance&nbsp;
on tired action movie tropes and its lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.840
of an emotional center, but even those who were&nbsp;
simply along for a thrill ride left disappointed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.840
Reviewer Jeff Mitchell struck the&nbsp;
killing blow with six words, writing:

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.400
"Boreshot feels like a better title."

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.320
You may be asking for it when you put the word&nbsp;
"lazy" right there in the title of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.240
and if the critics are to be believed,&nbsp;
Lazy Susan lives down to its name.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.080
Its simplistic plot follows a middle-aged&nbsp;
sad-sack, who one day decides to stop skating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.080
through life and make something of herself.&nbsp;
The fact that the character is played by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.760
man — Sean Hayes, of Will and Grace fame — should&nbsp;
begin to give you an idea of its many problems.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.560
Reviewers clocked the movie for its&nbsp;
stiff pacing, a lack of big laughs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.920
and the weird stunt casting of&nbsp;
Hayes that didn't add up to much.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.640
As Frank Scheck wrote for The Hollywood Reporter,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.120
"It's hard to imagine exactly what Sean Hayes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.320
was thinking [...] Lazy Susan&nbsp;
simply feels like a mistake."

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.200
"Are you jealous, Velvet&nbsp;
Swinson?" "I am definitely not."

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.400
Inheritance is a film with a strong cast and an&nbsp;
amazing premise. After the death of her wealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.000
father, a woman is directed to a secret bunker&nbsp;
on the family's property, where a mysterious man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.600
claims he was held captive for three decades. It's&nbsp;
a scintillating setup, but most critics felt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:05.360
movie was terminally slow and boring, failing&nbsp;
to live up to its totally bonkers potential.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.520
"Who are you?" "I'd like to think&nbsp;
I'm a member of the family."

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.760
Calling the screenplay "intelligence-deprived,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.600
Rolling Stone critic Peter Travers savaged&nbsp;
director Vaughn Stein in his review, writing,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.200
"His misbegotten and, yes,&nbsp;
preposterous new movie hits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.280
a new low that will test the patience&nbsp;
and the sanity of anyone who sees it."

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.760
Bruce Willis is a national treasure,&nbsp;
but he's spent a nice stretch of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.440
career now doing subpar work&nbsp;
in some pretty terrible movies.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.800
One 2020 low point is his&nbsp;
actioner Survive the Night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.080
which sees him portraying a retired cop called&nbsp;
in to save the day when the family of his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.040
a surgeon, gets held hostage by a pair of&nbsp;
robbers, demanding medical attention after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.160
one is wounded during a heist. It's a kind&nbsp;
of silly premise, and it's executed horribly.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.040
"If my brother dies, I'm gonna kill you."

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.560
Jeffrey M. Anderson of Common Sense Media&nbsp;
took everyone involved with Survive the Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.520
to task with a review which was probably more&nbsp;
entertaining than the movie itself. He wrote,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.120
"This movie looks and sounds like a&nbsp;
real thriller, but the premise and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.680
characters' behaviors and interactions are so&nbsp;
stunningly absurd that it's nearly impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.240
to turn your brain off and enjoy. [...] This&nbsp;
movie is so bad it will leave you gobsmacked."

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.360
The Last Days of American Crime must&nbsp;
have sounded great as an elevator pitch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.400
As the government prepares to broadcast a signal&nbsp;
that will rewire the brains of the populace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.120
making it impossible for them to commit&nbsp;
crimes, one lowly gangster who never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.640
managed a big score puts together a team to&nbsp;
try to pull off... well, the last crime ever.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.840
If that plot sounds comic book-y to you, you won't&nbsp;
be surprised to learn that it's based on a graphic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.680
novel by comics legend Rick Remender. But director&nbsp;
Olivier Megaton and his cast of up-and-comers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.320
couldn't make the pieces fit together. The&nbsp;
flick did pull off one amazing feat, however:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.840
with a zero percent score on Rotten Tomatoes, it&nbsp;
failed to notch even a single positive review.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.080
Based on a series of YA novels by Eoin Colfer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.640
Artemis Fowl arrived on Disney+ as one&nbsp;
of the worst-reviewed films of the year.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.360
"It would appear we've garnered&nbsp;
quite a bit of interest, Artie."

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.560
Writing for RogerEbert.com, Christy Lemire&nbsp;
called Artemis Fowl "a glossy and empty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.920
exercise in world-building," lamenting the movie's&nbsp;
razzle-dazzle lack of substance. As she put it,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.240
"There's always another fearsome creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.080
another giant set piece or fight&nbsp;
scene, with a frantic score urging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.320
the action along. There's a great deal of&nbsp;
exertion on display but very little magic."

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.720
The film's breakneck pace came under fire from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.320
nearly everyone who watched it,&nbsp;
with writer Rich Cline noting,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.880
"It's basically a three-hour script crammed&nbsp;
into 90 minutes, so nothing quite lands."

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.080
Most ominous for its young star Ferdia Shaw —&nbsp;
the grandson of late, legendary actor Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.480
Shaw — was the assessment of News-Gazette&nbsp;
writer Charles Koplinski, who wrote,

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.920
"Artemis Fowl is the sort of&nbsp;
movie that kills careers."

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:56.800
"Good to know."

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.720
The indie mystery Darkness Falls&nbsp;
has an interesting enough setup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.760
with a detective coming to suspect that his late&nbsp;
wife, who supposedly killed herself, was in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.280
killed by somebody else. Digging for answers, he&nbsp;
finds himself on the trail of a father-and-son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.640
serial killer team driven by their hatred of&nbsp;
women. The movie tries to rise above cliches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.040
and say something about society -- and that's&nbsp;
where critics say it falls flat on its face.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.240
Sheila O'Malley of RogerEbert.com pointed&nbsp;
out the obvious problem with the preachy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.560
aspirations of Darkness Falls in her&nbsp;
blistering one-star review, writing,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.040
"The attempt to create a 'serious'&nbsp;
anti-misogyny message through this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.080
shallow, improbable material&nbsp;
— directed and written by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.040
two men, with no complex female characters&nbsp;
onscreen — is maybe the worst part of it."

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.160
Rich Cline of Shadows on the Wall went even&nbsp;
farther, saying the movie's over-serious tone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.760
lack of subtlety ended up making it far funnier of&nbsp;
a viewing experience than the filmmakers intended.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:59.360
The sophomore feature from writer, director,&nbsp;
and former Daily Show host Jon Stewart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.080
one might reasonably expect Irresistible&nbsp;
to be a blistering political satire.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.400
Unfortunately, the tale of a small-town&nbsp;
election that unexpectedly steals the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.000
national spotlight turned out&nbsp;
to be an unfunny disappointment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.600
as critics found the film to be&nbsp;
surprisingly lacking in bite.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.600
"Can I be completely honest with you?&nbsp;
No bull." "Be nice -- my daughter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.000
here." "That was your daughter&nbsp;
with her arm up the... cow."

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.600
Nick Schager of The Daily Beast&nbsp;
wrote in a negative review,

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.240
"[The film is] content to indulge in&nbsp;
condescending clichés and cornball humor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:36.160
In desperate need of ruthless&nbsp;
incisiveness, it goes soft at every turn."

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:42.160
Desperados seemed to have all of the elements for&nbsp;
a successful rom-com with a hilarious premise:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.480
A woman leaves an embarrassing, drunken&nbsp;
message on her new boyfriend's phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.920
and must go to extreme lengths&nbsp;
to erase it. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.240
the final product ended up pandering,&nbsp;
far-fetched, and borderline offensive.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.920
"Would it be insane if I went to&nbsp;
Mexico and erased the email?" "Yes."

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.120
Tomris Laffly of RogerEbert.com&nbsp;
was among many critics who called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.360
the flick out for its disappointing&nbsp;
over-reliance on lowbrow humor, writing,

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.480
"This critic always craves&nbsp;
stories centered on females&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:18.400
in meaty roles being unapologetically messy&nbsp;
and problematic. But maybe there is a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.240
not make it look and feel this&nbsp;
much like an insult to women."

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.720
Detroit News' Adam Graham simply wanted&nbsp;
a little more effort from the screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.840
calling it out for its many cliches, "painful&nbsp;
set pieces" and "strained life lessons."

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.000
For a film ostensibly celebrating the&nbsp;
power of female friendship, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:39.920
the most towering insult came from&nbsp;
FilmWeek's Amy Nicholson, who wrote,

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.000
"This movie made me embarrassed to be a woman."

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.360
David Ayer has had a rough few years, critically&nbsp;
speaking. After writing well-regarded flicks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.320
like Training Day, and directing End of&nbsp;
Watch and Fury, he turned in the massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:58.160
clunker that was 2016's Suicide Squad and&nbsp;
the successful-but-critically-reviled Bright.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.520
His latest effort, The Tax Collector, finds&nbsp;
Bobby Soto and Shia LeBeouf starring as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.240
low-level enforcers for a drug kingpin, who&nbsp;
run afoul of their employer's competition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.080
The story makes for a loose framework upon&nbsp;
which is built a never-ending series of clichés.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:19.280
Carlos Aguilar of the Los Angeles Times called&nbsp;
the film "one of the most atrocious viewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:24.320
experiences of the year," and called it out&nbsp;
for being a, quote, "flagrantly violent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.120
stereotype ridden, poorly realized production."

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:33.200
While the movie was a hit for Netflix, critics&nbsp;
agree -- there's a lot wrong with The Wrong Missy.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.640
Lead actor David Spade was singled out early and&nbsp;
often for his lack of enthusiasm in the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.920
with AV Club's Ignatiy Vishnevetsky describing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.000
as having "the screen presence of&nbsp;
an unenthused real estate agent."

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.320
Jonathan Wilson of Ready Steady&nbsp;
Cut likewise slammed Spade's work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:56.240
but was equally unimpressed with his co-star&nbsp;
Lauren Lapkus, who throws everything she's got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.200
into bringing an immensely annoying character to&nbsp;
life. The results are painful. The critic wrote,

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.520
"No right-minded agent would present&nbsp;
a client with a script as diabolical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.520
as [this] one ... except apparently&nbsp;
whoever mismanages Lapkus's career."

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.680
Revenge thrillers are awesome,&nbsp;
and Terry Crews is awesome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.760
so one would expect John Henry — which&nbsp;
stars Crews as a gentle giant forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.480
to go on the bloody warpath — to at&nbsp;
least have been... watchable. Alas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:27.760
the movie ended up as one of the rare films to&nbsp;
draw a complete goose egg on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:33.600
with critics unanimously agreeing that for all&nbsp;
its ambitions, the movie is kind of ridiculous.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.760
The New York Times' Jeanette Catsoulis&nbsp;
called the film "A thug-life thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.520
so frequently preposterous that&nbsp;
it almost resembles a parody."

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.480
Most of the film's problems were laid at the&nbsp;
feet of first-time writer-director Will Forbes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:51.520
who seemed to have little idea about how to&nbsp;
handle his star's towering screen presence.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.920
Wrote Dominic Griffin of Spectrum Culture,

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.120
"John Henry is both uninspired and uninspiring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.680
a mixed bag and missed opportunity that's&nbsp;
as confounding as it is infuriating."

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.480
The Vanished is a thriller stocked&nbsp;
with stars who have seen better days;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.000
its main cast includes Thomas Jane, Anne Heche,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.600
and Jason Patric, and they all give off the&nbsp;
distinct vibe that they'd rather be anywhere else.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.920
Critics called out the flick's acting, writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:21.600
and direction, but most crucially,&nbsp;
The Vanished is a film that relies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.720
solely on a third-act twist that nearly&nbsp;
every observer found to be completely absurd.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:30.480
Jane and Heche star as a couple whose&nbsp;
daughter disappears on a camping trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.240
delivering unconvincing performances&nbsp;
en route to a big reveal.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:38.240
Carla Hay of Culture Mix addressed&nbsp;
the script's fatal flaws, writing,

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.560
"The plot twist is meant to elevate the&nbsp;
story, but it just ends up sinking the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.680
which was already drowning in&nbsp;
a swamp of implausibility."

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:48.960
Critic Mark Dujsik rejected&nbsp;
the movie completely, saying,

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.480
"The Vanished is wildly and weirdly incompetent,&nbsp;
featuring one miscalculation after another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:58.160
after another, and after another.&nbsp;
It's an embarrassment of a movie."

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.680
Bruce Willis returns to the list with another&nbsp;
late-career detour into the kinds of movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.480
you find in a gas station bargain bin.&nbsp;
This time, he's headlining Hard Kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:13.520
a brain-dead thriller which managed to&nbsp;
"achieve" a 0 percent rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:17.920
The plot, such as it is, involves a group&nbsp;
of mercenaries trying to bring down a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.480
mad computer hacker before he can start&nbsp;
World War III, but that hardly matters;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:27.600
every single critic tasked with viewing it had&nbsp;
nothing but massive volumes of scorn to heap on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.880
the film from director Matt Eskandari, who also&nbsp;
directed Willis in the reviled Survive the Night.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:36.640
Reviewers seemed to have a lot more fun&nbsp;
writing about Hard Kill than they did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.600
watching it. The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter's Frank Scheck wrote,

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:45.040
"[Willis] seems to have settled on making listless&nbsp;
appearances in forgettable B-movie action movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:49.680
as a retirement funding plan... the&nbsp;
unimaginatively titled Hard Kill was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.600
filmed in a mere ten days, making you&nbsp;
wonder how they spent eight of them."

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:58.400
Frank Swietek of One Guy's Opinion went even&nbsp;
pithier with his assessment, proclaiming,

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:03.440
"The title of this noisy, stupid, totally&nbsp;
boring bomb should really be Hard Watch."

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:08.320
Money Plane is a pretty stupid action&nbsp;
movie that features Frasier star Kelsey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:13.440
Grammer as a sinister crime boss named "The&nbsp;
Rumble." That's kind of all you need to know.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:18.080
"I'm the baddest motherf----- on&nbsp;
the planet. I am Darius Grouch III,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:22.480
The Rumble, and I am taking down the&nbsp;
money plane. Now bring me my money!"

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:27.120
This ludicrous tale of a heist&nbsp;
involving a flying high-stakes casino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.040
baffled critics in 2020, including&nbsp;
Nick Schager of The Daily Beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:34.400
who simply couldn't wrap his head&nbsp;
around the movie's absurd premise.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:37.600
Meanwhile, Peter Sobczynski of&nbsp;
eFilm Critic was disappointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.720
that the movie didn't lean more&nbsp;
into that absurdity. He wrote,

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.680
"Look, I do not object to the fact&nbsp;
that Money Plane is trying to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:47.600
the most self-consciously dopey movie&nbsp;
of recent memory. What I object to is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:52.400
that the whole thing is too stupid and lazy to&nbsp;
accomplish even that not-exactly-lofty goal."

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:58.320
Everybody loves Ron Perlman. There's nobody&nbsp;
better at playing gruff, badass types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:03.200
he's got a great sense of humor, and he loves&nbsp;
engaging fans on social media. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:07.360
he failed to engage anyone with Run with&nbsp;
the Hunted, in which he plays a private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:12.160
investigator and mentor to a woman determined&nbsp;
to find the boy who saved her life as a child.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:17.520
From that seemingly innocuous premise springs&nbsp;
a bizarrely violent picture which doesn't quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.600
seem to know what kind of film it wants to&nbsp;
be — or, indeed, if it should have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:27.680
film at all. Run with the Hunted is another&nbsp;
2020 inductee in Rotten Tomatoes' 0% club&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:32.960
with not a lot to recommend it. As Peter&nbsp;
Canavese of the Celluloid Dreams podcast opined,

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:37.520
"[The movie] feels like a TV season's worth&nbsp;
of script had to be hacked down to 90 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:39.840
resulting in incoherent near-pointlessness."

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:46.480
Nobody ever accused the self-help book The&nbsp;
Secret of being ripe for a rom-com adaptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:49.760
but that didn't prevent veteran&nbsp;
genre director Andy Tennant from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:52.320
giving it a shot with The Secret: Dare to Dream.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.080
Assembling a surprisingly appealing&nbsp;
cast which includes Katie Holmes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:00.560
Jerry O' Connell, and Josh Lucas, Tennant&nbsp;
shoehorned the book's message of...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:06.080
well, achieving your heart's desires by wishing&nbsp;
really hard into a maudlin romance that wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:10.000
have worked even without its connection&nbsp;
to its questionable source material.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.640
In what amounts to a critical napalming,&nbsp;
Adam Graham of Detroit News wrote,

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:21.120
"The Secret: Dare to Dream is a gauze-covered,&nbsp;
soft-peddled, vaguely spiritual tale of cosmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:26.400
happenstance [...] innocent and innocuous enough&nbsp;
to make Nicholas Sparks look like David Lynch."

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:29.600
Writing for Decider, Jade&nbsp;
Budowski acknowledged that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:33.280
the movie steers more toward&nbsp;
comedy than wisdom, writing,

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:36.240
"For a movie intended to be inspiring, there is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:40.160
little about this that feels genuinely&nbsp;
uplifting that it's almost comical."

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.160
David Bowie is one of the most&nbsp;
remarkable and fascinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:49.200
entertainers of all time, totally deserving&nbsp;
of a movie that showcases his life. One day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:52.800
somebody may make that movie.&nbsp;
Until then, there's Stardust.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:57.920
The movie is about a brief period in the life of&nbsp;
the rock star starring Johnny Flynn, an actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:02.720
musician who bears so little a resemblance&nbsp;
to Bowie that it's actually distracting.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:08.080
Stardust specifically concerns a&nbsp;
young Bowie's American tour in 1971&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:12.400
and his creation of the Ziggy Stardust&nbsp;
persona. Actually, Stardust can't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:17.600
really claim to be that, because at the outset,&nbsp;
audiences are informed that it's a speculative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:22.560
work of primarily fiction — and it's a giant&nbsp;
disappointment unworthy of its inspiration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:28.080
As a final insult, filmmakers also failed to&nbsp;
secure the rights to any of Bowie's songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:32.720
making the movie, as Barry Hertz of The Globe&nbsp;
and Mail put it, "a pointless exercise."

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:37.920
There are a few things about The War with&nbsp;
Grandpa that are more than a little unsettling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:41.360
The film suggests that family&nbsp;
bonds are extremely fragile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:45.680
liable to collapse under the weight of the&nbsp;
pettiest of squabbles. It's a strained and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:50.560
labored comedy that makes it hard for the audience&nbsp;
to root for anyone — or laugh at much of anything.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:54.400
Tweenage Peter is so entitled that&nbsp;
he completely lacks any sympathy or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:58.320
empathy whatsoever for Grandpa Ed, and&nbsp;
decides to start a prank war with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:02.880
old guy, despite the fact that he's a&nbsp;
grieving, increasingly frail widower.

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:07.840
Meanwhile, Grandpa Ed is a veteran, and&nbsp;
not one to back down, which leads him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:13.760
escalate the prank war against his grandson in&nbsp;
a way that you hope no real person ever would.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:18.960
Insubstantial and unlikable, Peter Travers of&nbsp;
Good Morning America calls The War with Grandpa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:23.360
"barely passable piffle," while&nbsp;
Bilge Ebiri, writing for Vulture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:27.840
says that the movie, quote, "seems to vanish&nbsp;
from the mind even as you're watching it."

